        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why does my Echelon Stride keep stopping?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Echelon's support centre lists four checks: update the app and firmware, make sure the belt is aligned and not too loose, check the belt runs normally with nobody on it, and remove any extension cord. Its manuals also say GFCI and AFCI outlets can make treadmills shut down, so use a plain grounded outlet, ideally on a dedicated circuit.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it worth fixing an Echelon treadmill that shuts off?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually, because the common causes are free to fix: the outlet, an extension cord, belt tension and out-of-date firmware. If those checks fail, Echelon says parts are likely needed. Stride treadmills bought on or after 4 November 2026 carry a lifetime motor warranty for the original purchaser, so contact Echelon before paying for any repair.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What do the Echelon treadmill error codes mean?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              In the Stride-6 and Stride-6s manuals, E00 is no safety-key signal, E01 a board connection fault, E02 overload, E03 no speed-sensor signal, E04 an incline fault, E05 over-current and E06 a controller self-check error. E07, E08 and E09 call for a new lower control board. Check the outlet first for E02 and E05.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I reset my Echelon treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Remove the safety key, switch off, unplug from the wall, wait at least 30 seconds, then plug in, switch on and reinsert the key. For an incline fault or E04, remove the key and hold Start and Stop together: the Stride-6 manual says more than 3 seconds, and Echelon's maintenance article says 5 to 8 seconds.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why are the heart rate pads on my Echelon treadmill not working?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Grip pads need both hands held firmly for about 5 seconds, and Echelon's manual says movement affects accuracy, so they often drop out while running. Clean the pads and try again at a walk. On the Stride-6 and 6s, the manual says to wear a heart rate monitor, paired through the heart icon in the Echelon Fit app.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How often should I lubricate an Echelon treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The Stride-6, Stride-6s and Stride Sport manuals say every 60 days for light use, 45 for medium and 30 for heavy, using half a bottle of silicone oil. Echelon's support article says every 3 to 5 months or 500 miles. The original Stride has a waxed belt and should not be lubricated at all.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I use an Echelon treadmill without a membership?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. On 26 November 2026, Echelon's Stride-6 page said manual workouts from the console always work without a membership, and its support centre says equipment runs in Freestyle mode without a subscription. Classes and the longer Premier warranty need an active Premier membership.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
